WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.520
Why do dining philosophers deadlock?

2
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.100
Each philosopher needs two neighboring

3
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.440
chopsticks, but each chopstick can

4
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.780
be held by only one

5
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.660
philosopher at a time. A

6
00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.120
locally sensible first acquisition can

7
00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.200
create a circular dependency when

8
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.600
everyone follows it together.

9
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:19.740
There are five philosophers and

10
00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.480
five chopsticks around the table.

11
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.340
Model each chopstick with a

12
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.300
binary semaphore initially one. Philosopher

13
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.580
i waits for chopstick i

14
00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:28.880
and then chopstick i plus

15
00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.660
one modulo five, eats, and

16
00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:31.800
eventually releases both.

17
00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.320
Let P zero acquire its

18
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.000
first chopstick and then be

19
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.520
paused. P one acquires its

20
00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.360
first, followed by P two

21
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.520
and P three. None yet

22
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.020
has the second resource needed

23
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.040
to eat, but each successful

24
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.740
acquisition consumes an available chopstick.

25
00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.460
Finally P four acquires the

26
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.860
last remaining first chopstick. Now

27
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:55.320
all five resources are held,

28
00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.080
one by each philosopher. Every

29
00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.540
next request targets a stick

30
00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:00.780
already held by a neighbor,

31
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.540
completing the circular wait.

32
00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.360
All five block on their

33
00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.320
second acquisition, and none reaches

34
00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.060
the release code. A processor

35
00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.040
scheduler cannot dispatch a blocked

36
00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:11.660
philosopher into releasing a resource

37
00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.020
it has not finished using.

38
00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.500
Fairness about who runs does

39
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.260
not repair the acquisition cycle.

40
00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.560
One remedy limits simultaneous contenders

41
00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.220
to four using another counting

42
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.100
semaphore. With at most four

43
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.260
philosophers attempting to acquire, the

44
00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.340
five-person circular hold pattern cannot

45
00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:28.520
form in the same way.

46
00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.520
A permitted philosopher can obtain

47
00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:31.880
the missing second stick and

48
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:32.520
make progress.

49
00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:35.160
Another remedy takes both required

50
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.360
chopsticks together as an indivisible

51
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.760
allocation decision, or waits without

52
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.100
holding either. That removes hold

53
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:43.840
and wait, but the implementation

54
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.080
must protect the both-or-none decision

55
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.520
correctly. Two unrelated successful-looking tests

56
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:50.960
do not make it atomic.

57
00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.600
A global resource ordering is

58
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.060
also effective: always acquire the

59
00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.320
lower-numbered chopstick before the higher-numbered

60
00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.640
one. The philosopher between zero

61
00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.160
and four must request zero

62
00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.360
first, breaking the circular direction.

63
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.300
An odd-even asymmetric ordering can

64
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.920
similarly prevent the all-same-direction cycle.

65
00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.320
Proving the absence of deadlock

66
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.420
does not automatically prove that

67
00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:16.320
every philosopher receives a turn

68
00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:17.880
within a fixed bound. A

69
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.580
poorly chosen permission or queue

70
00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:20.800
rule may still starve a

71
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.400
particular philosopher. Keep resource safety,

72
00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.380
deadlock freedom and fairness as

73
00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:26.120
separate claims.

74
00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:28.040
Things to take note of.

75
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.280
Show the five first acquisitions,

76
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.500
identify the cycle, and explain

77
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.520
which deadlock condition your remedy

78
00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.660
breaks. More hardware or a

79
00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.300
different quantum does not itself

80
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.900
change the order in which

81
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.920
this broken protocol requests chopsticks.
